/**
 * Inline SVG brand icons.
 *
 * Font Awesome shipped two webfonts from cdnjs: fa-solid-900 at 155 KB and
 * fa-brands-400 at 116 KB, and on Slow 4G they did not finish until 7.9s. The
 * brands file existed for exactly eight logos. Those eight are inlined here, so
 * nothing on the site references a brands class any more and the browser --
 * which only downloads a webfont when a glyph actually needs it -- never
 * requests that 116 KB file at all.
 *
 * The 183 solid and regular icons are deliberately left on the webfont.
 * Hand-authoring that many paths is a lot of surface for subtle drift in shape
 * or alignment, and the instruction was to leave anything uncertain alone.
 *
 * The <i> wrapper is kept. Stylesheets across the site target the element
 * itself -- ".ct-info-list li i", ".ab-card-header i", ".number-icon i" and
 * others set margin, colour and font-size on it -- and swapping in a bare <svg>
 * would drop every one of those rules. The <i> stays, the glyph moves inside it.
 *
 * Sizing matches the webfont rather than approximating it: height is 1em, width
 * is the icon's own viewBox ratio (Font Awesome draws on a 512-unit em square),
 * and vertical-align is -0.125em, which is what Font Awesome itself applies.
 * fill="currentColor" means existing colour rules keep working untouched.
 *
 * Paths are Font Awesome Free 6.7.2, the version already loaded, so the shapes
 * are identical rather than lookalikes. (CC BY 4.0, fontawesome.com/license)
 */

